# Spec 025 — Site Restructure: LiveNear and DriveCheap

Status: Draft
Owner: <you>
Related: GitHub #15; 010 (branding, target sizes, one h1), 018 (LiveNear
name and assets), 022 R1 (routing, partly superseded here), 021 (token guard)

## Overview

The site solves two separate problems but presents them as a map with a
car tool hidden at an unlinked URL. This spec gives each its own named
product, **LiveNear** (home prices and commutes) and **DriveCheap** (cost of
owning a car in Norway), under a new landing page, a way to switch between
them, and one visual identity based on LiveNear's.

## Context

The map and `/cars` share no shell and no links in either direction, and
cars has its own palette. The map parses its URL once at module load, so
leaving and returning restores stale state; that becomes reachable as soon
as navigation between products exists. The server only falls back to the
SPA under `/cars`, so any new path needs a server route.

## Requirements

### Routes

- **R1 — Product URLs.** `/` is a landing page. The map is at `/live-near`.
  The car tool is at `/drive-cheap`, with its existing sub-routes
  (`/market`, `/checklist`, `/compare`, `/compare/curves`,
  `/option/:optionId`) beneath it. This supersedes the routing paths and
  "`/` stays the map" in 022 R1; the rest of 022 R1 stands.
- **R2 — Old map links.** `/` with at least one valid map parameter (as
  recognised by `parseAppUrl`) replaces to `/live-near` with the query
  intact. `/` with no valid map parameter shows the landing page.
- **R3 — Old car links.** Any `/cars` or `/cars/...` URL replaces to the
  same location under `/drive-cheap`, preserving percent-encoding, query
  (including `?c=` and `?mock=1`) and hash. Matching is case-insensitive.
- **R4 — Unknown paths.** In the app, any other non-`/api` path replaces
  to `/`.
- **R5 — Server fallback.** A hard reload of `/live-near`, or of any path
  under `/drive-cheap` or `/cars`, serves the SPA. Static assets and `/api`
  are unaffected, and other unknown paths still return 404 from the
  server. Fallback registration is testable without importing the
  production app.
- **R6 — Code loading.** The landing page does not download the map or car
  bundles until needed; both products load on demand, and are preloaded when
  the user hovers or focuses a link to them. A failed chunk shows the
  existing error card. Map popup styling is unchanged by the new CSS order.

### Moving between products

- **R7 — Product switcher.** Both products show a switcher linking to
  LiveNear and DriveCheap, marking the current one, inside the map's
  existing panel and the DriveCheap header. It adds no bar that reduces
  map height, stays visible when the mobile panel is collapsed, and meets
  010 R5 target sizes. Each product links back to the landing page through
  its logo.
- **R8 — State survives switching.** Leaving a product and returning within
  the same page load restores its latest URL state, including for a `?c=`
  link never edited. Last-used URLs are held in memory only and are never
  written to storage, because map URLs contain work coordinates.
- **R9 — No cross-product URL writes.** A pending map or car URL update
  never writes onto another route. Pending car saves are flushed, not lost,
  when leaving DriveCheap.
- **R10 — Location prompt.** The map geolocates only on its first mount in
  a page load with no recognised parameters. Returning to the map never
  re-prompts.

### Landing page

- **R11 — Landing content.** The landing page names both products, says in
  one sentence each what problem it solves, and links to each, with the
  coverage stated honestly (LiveNear: United States; DriveCheap: Norway).
  Exactly one h1. It works at phone width and without the map bundle.
  The page reads "LiveNear & DriveCheap" as the site name.

### Identity

- **R12 — Names.** The car product reads DriveCheap everywhere user-facing,
  and LiveNear stays one word (018 R1). The title is set per route: landing
  "LiveNear & DriveCheap", map "LiveNear", cars "DriveCheap". The crash card
  uses copy that is correct for any product.
- **R13 — Logos.** A matching SVG mark and wordmark exist for each product,
  in the shared palette and legible at panel-header size. Claude drafts
  them; **the owner approves them before they replace any current asset.**
  The map pin (`mark-512.png`) keeps its art.
- **R14 — Shared palette.** One token set in `styles.css` drives both
  products. It adds `--accent: #2b6fa8` for text and primary controls, since
  `#3182bd` is about 4.15:1 on white. Car tokens derive from shared tokens,
  except where a distinct meaning exists (estimate/live status, stronger
  borders). Car charts and CSS have no remaining colour literals outside
  the token block, and the car screens no longer name the unloaded Inter font.
- **R15 — Metadata.** `og:url` and `og:image` use
  `https://livenear.onrender.com`, replacing the suspended service, and the
  description covers both products. CLAUDE.md and README describe the new
  routes and names.

### Continuity

- **R16 — Unchanged internals.** `/api/cars`, the `cars` folder names,
  `livenear.*` storage keys (so saved profiles survive), the map's and car
  tool's URL parameters, data files and engine are unchanged.

## Non-functional / Constraints

- No personal data is persisted server-side, and no new storage of map URLs
  (R8). The hard constraints in CLAUDE.md are unchanged.
- No new runtime dependency.

## Acceptance Criteria

- [ ] `/` shows the landing page; `/?state=WA` lands on `/live-near?state=WA`
      and `/?state=XX` stays on the landing page (R1, R2, R11).
- [ ] `/cars/option/a%2Fb?c=x#y` lands on `/drive-cheap/option/a%2Fb?c=x#y`
      (R3).
- [ ] An unknown app path lands on `/` (R4).
- [ ] Reloading `/live-near`, `/drive-cheap/market` and `/cars/compare` on
      the production build serves the app; a static asset still serves and
      `/nope` returns 404 (R5).
- [ ] The built `dist/index.html` does not reference MapLibre (R6), and the
      landing page's network log shows no map chunk until a link is used.
- [ ] Map popups look as they did before lazy loading (R6).
- [ ] The switcher appears on both products, in the collapsed mobile panel,
      and at 44 px target size; the map height is unchanged (R7).
- [ ] With map and car state set, switching back and forth restores both,
      with no location prompt on return (R8, R10).
- [ ] A map or car update pending during a route change does not alter the
      new route's URL, and a pending car save is kept (R9).
- [ ] Tab titles and crash-card copy are correct on each route (R12).
- [ ] The owner has approved logos, and they are in place (R13).
- [ ] No colour literals remain outside the token block in car CSS and
      charts; primary controls pass 4.5:1 contrast (R14).
- [ ] Shared links show the current origin in metadata (R15).
- [ ] A saved profile from before this change still loads (R16).
- [ ] `npm run test`, `npm run build`, `pytest`, `ruff check .` pass, and CI
      smoke-tests `/live-near`, `/drive-cheap/market` and `/cars/compare`.

## Out of Scope (explicit)

- A custom domain; renaming the Render service or GitHub repository.
- Per-product social cards or favicons.
- New car or map features, and Norway on the map (GitHub #14).
- Internal renames of `cars` to DriveCheap in code, or of the API.

## Verification notes

R1-R4, R9 → `routes.test.tsx` and writer-guard tests; R5 →
`test_spa_fallback.py` and CI smoke; R6 → build check plus a visual popup
check; R7, R8, R10, R11 → by hand in the container build at desktop and
phone widths; R12-R15 → visual and grep sweep; R16 → load a saved profile
and run existing suites. The human verifies; the implementing agent does
not self-certify done.
